    {
      "id": "JbQd-IenSwI89ZDRoOG0P",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1760.1883727379522,
      "y": 3914.487352945048,
      "width": 797.7952025196778,
      "height": 2125,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b01",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1589923310,
      "version": 2094,
      "versionNonce": 1346836209,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1779893273416,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) How can we ensure short urls are unique?\n\n\nRequirements:\n- Short codes unique.\n- Short codes short as possible\n- Short codes efficiently generated.\n\n\nBAD SOLUTION: Long Url Prefix\n\n→ Use a fixed prefix of the input url as the short code\n→ Take first N (eg: 8) characters of url and use as short code.\nwww.linkedin.com/in/evan-king-40072280/  In this case www.short.ly/www.link\n\nChallenges\n→ Fails uniqueness constraint\nwww.linkedin.com/in/evan-king-40072280/ & www.linkedin.com/in/stefanmai/ both\nmap to www.short.ly/www.link\n\n\n\nGREAT SOLUTION: Hash Function\n\n→ Random number generator (RNG) to create short codes. Generates random number\neach time a new URL is shortened & used as short code\n→ Can use common RNG fns like JS's Math.random() or more robust cryptographic RNG\nfns for increased unpredictability\n→ RNGs don't provide enough entropy to ensure short codes are unique\n\n→ Use a hash function like SHA-256 to generate a fixed-size hash code instead\n→ Pure hash functions are deterministic: long URL always maps to same short code\nwithout needing to query DB\n→ May not be desirable (multiple codes per URL or want to prevent\nguessability/adversarial preimages), For latter, can add a secret salt or nonce (HMAC)\n→ Take the output and encode it using a base62 encoding scheme & take first N chars\nas short code. N is determined based on the number of characters needed to minimize\ncollisions (e.g., 8 characters gives 62^8 ≈ 218 trillion possible codes).\n\n\nChallenges\n→ There's still chance of generating duplicate short codes as the number of stored\nURLs increases.\n→ At large scale this can become non-negligible, requiring retries and database checks\nto enforce uniqueness\n\n→ To reduce collisions, we need higher entropy, which means generating longer short\ncodes. However, longer codes negate the benefit of having a short URL.\n→ Detecting and resolving collisions also adds database lookups on insertion,\nintroducing latency and complexity. This creates a tradeoff between uniqueness,\nshortness, and efficiency—making it difficult to optimize all three simultaneously.\n\n→ To handle collisions, implement a UNIQUE constraint on the short code column and\nretry with bounded attempts (e.g., max 3-5 retries) before falling back to a different\nstrategy or returning an error.\n→ Upon saving to the database, we'll get an error if the short code already exists. In this\ncase, we can simply retry the process with a random salt added to the hash function.\n\n\n\nGREAT SOLUTION: Unique Counter with Base62 Encoding\n\n→ \"Avoids collisions\" by incrementing a counter for each new url, & encoding it using\nbase62 to ensure compacted representation\n→ Each counter value is unique, eliminating risk of collisions without needing additional\nchecks\n→ Incrementing a counter and encoding it is computationally efficient, supporting high\nthroughput\n→ With proper distributed counter management, system can scale horizontally to\nhandle massive numbers of URLs\n\n→ Short code can be decoded back to the original ID, for database lookups\n\n\nChallenges\n→ Maintaining single distributed global counter,  can be difficult due to synchronization\nissues\n→ Sequential counters produce predictable short codes, making URL enumeration\npossible. Attackers can iterate through codes to discover all URLs.\n→ Can be overcome by applying a reversible transformation (like XOR with a secret key)\nbefore base62 encoding, or accept the tradeoff since short URLs are often meant to be\nshared publicly anyway\n\n→ Size of short code increases over time with this method\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) How can we ensure short urls are unique?\n\n\nRequirements:\n- Short codes unique.\n- Short codes short as possible\n- Short codes efficiently generated.\n\n\nBAD SOLUTION: Long Url Prefix\n\n→ Use a fixed prefix of the input url as the short code\n→ Take first N (eg: 8) characters of url and use as short code. www.linkedin.com/in/evan-king-40072280/  In this case www.short.ly/www.link\n\nChallenges\n→ Fails uniqueness constraint\nwww.linkedin.com/in/evan-king-40072280/ & www.linkedin.com/in/stefanmai/ both map to www.short.ly/www.link\n\n\n\nGREAT SOLUTION: Hash Function\n\n→ Random number generator (RNG) to create short codes. Generates random number each time a new URL is shortened & used as short code\n→ Can use common RNG fns like JS's Math.random() or more robust cryptographic RNG fns for increased unpredictability\n→ RNGs don't provide enough entropy to ensure short codes are unique\n\n→ Use a hash function like SHA-256 to generate a fixed-size hash code instead\n→ Pure hash functions are deterministic: long URL always maps to same short code without needing to query DB\n→ May not be desirable (multiple codes per URL or want to prevent guessability/adversarial preimages), For latter, can add a secret salt or nonce (HMAC)\n→ Take the output and encode it using a base62 encoding scheme & take first N chars as short code. N is determined based on the number of characters needed to minimize collisions (e.g., 8 characters gives 62^8 ≈ 218 trillion possible codes).\n\n\nChallenges\n→ There's still chance of generating duplicate short codes as the number of stored URLs increases.\n→ At large scale this can become non-negligible, requiring retries and database checks to enforce uniqueness\n\n→ To reduce collisions, we need higher entropy, which means generating longer short codes. However, longer codes negate the benefit of having a short URL.\n→ Detecting and resolving collisions also adds database lookups on insertion, introducing latency and complexity. This creates a tradeoff between uniqueness, shortness, and efficiency—making it difficult to optimize all three simultaneously.\n\n→ To handle collisions, implement a UNIQUE constraint on the short code column and retry with bounded attempts (e.g., max 3-5 retries) before falling back to a different strategy or returning an error.\n→ Upon saving to the database, we'll get an error if the short code already exists. In this case, we can simply retry the process with a random salt added to the hash function.\n\n\n\nGREAT SOLUTION: Unique Counter with Base62 Encoding\n\n→ \"Avoids collisions\" by incrementing a counter for each new url, & encoding it using base62 to ensure compacted representation\n→ Each counter value is unique, eliminating risk of collisions without needing additional checks\n→ Incrementing a counter and encoding it is computationally efficient, supporting high throughput\n→ With proper distributed counter management, system can scale horizontally to handle massive numbers of URLs\n\n→ Short code can be decoded back to the original ID, for database lookups\n\n\nChallenges\n→ Maintaining single distributed global counter,  can be difficult due to synchronization issues\n→ Sequential counters produce predictable short codes, making URL enumeration possible. Attackers can iterate through codes to discover all URLs.\n→ Can be overcome by applying a reversible transformation (like XOR with a secret key) before base62 encoding, or accept the tradeoff since short URLs are often meant to be shared publicly anyway\n\n→ Size of short code increases over time with this method\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "pIPigoQ79kMTSulNiV_td",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2715.3825961149314,
      "y": 3907.01826351818,
      "width": 797.7952025196778,
      "height": 1875,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
