      "seed": 1744675483,
      "version": 2417,
      "versionNonce": 1955643423,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1779887625206,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Why base62? 🤔\n\n→ Compact representation of numbers that uses 62 characters (a-z, A-Z, 0-9). 🔤\n→ It's 62 and not the more common base64 is because we exclude + and / - the slash is a\npath separator in URLs 🛤️ and the + can be interpreted as a space in query strings. 🚫\npythoninput_url = \"https://www.example.com/some/very/long/url\" 🔗\n\n# Canonicalize URL (lowercase host, strip default ports, normalize trailing slash, etc.) 🧹\ncanonical_url = canonicalize(input_url)\nhash_code = hash_function(canonical_url) #️⃣\nshort_code_encoded = base62_encode(hash_code) 🔢\nshort_code = short_code_encoded[:8] # 8 characters ✂️\n\n\nBase62 Encoding & Text Length\n\n→ Base62 does not shorten text — it almost always makes it longer\n→ Base62 is a representation format for numbers/binary, not a compression algorithm\n\n\nWhy text gets longer\n\n→ Plain ASCII already uses ~95 printable characters — more than Base62's 62\n→ Fewer symbols = more characters needed to represent the same data\n→ \"hello\" (5 chars) → Base62 → \"7tQLFHz\" (7 chars) ⚠️\n\nWhat Base62 is actually for\n\nUse case                                                 Why it helps\nRepresenting large integers              Shorter than Base10 or Base16\nURL tokens / short IDs                       URL-safe, compact vs raw numbers\nEncoding binary/hash data                Makes binary printable and URL-safe\n\n→ Input is always a number or raw binary — not human-readable text\n\n\n🗜️ If you want to actually shorten text\n\n→ Use a compression algorithm: gzip, zstd, brotli\n→ These find patterns and redundancy in content — Base62 is blind to structure entirely\n⚠️ Tradeoff: Encoding vs Compression\n→ Encoding (Base62) = changes representation, never reduces size of text\n→ Compression (gzip etc.) = reduces size by exploiting patterns, but output is binary (not\nhuman-readable)\n\n\nOne-liner\n\n→ Base62 makes numbers shorter to represent — applied to text it makes things longer.\nFor actual size reduction, use compression.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "y3IyYkIO3YRdtySwaxj2P",
      "originalText": "Why base62? 🤔\n\n→ Compact representation of numbers that uses 62 characters (a-z, A-Z, 0-9). 🔤\n→ It's 62 and not the more common base64 is because we exclude + and / - the slash is a path separator in URLs 🛤️ and the + can be interpreted as a space in query strings. 🚫\npythoninput_url = \"https://www.example.com/some/very/long/url\" 🔗\n\n# Canonicalize URL (lowercase host, strip default ports, normalize trailing slash, etc.) 🧹\ncanonical_url = canonicalize(input_url)\nhash_code = hash_function(canonical_url) #️⃣\nshort_code_encoded = base62_encode(hash_code) 🔢\nshort_code = short_code_encoded[:8] # 8 characters ✂️\n\n\nBase62 Encoding & Text Length\n\n→ Base62 does not shorten text — it almost always makes it longer\n→ Base62 is a representation format for numbers/binary, not a compression algorithm\n\n\nWhy text gets longer\n\n→ Plain ASCII already uses ~95 printable characters — more than Base62's 62\n→ Fewer symbols = more characters needed to represent the same data\n→ \"hello\" (5 chars) → Base62 → \"7tQLFHz\" (7 chars) ⚠️\n\nWhat Base62 is actually for\n\nUse case                                                 Why it helps\nRepresenting large integers              Shorter than Base10 or Base16\nURL tokens / short IDs                       URL-safe, compact vs raw numbers\nEncoding binary/hash data                Makes binary printable and URL-safe\n\n→ Input is always a number or raw binary — not human-readable text\n\n\n🗜️ If you want to actually shorten text\n\n→ Use a compression algorithm: gzip, zstd, brotli\n→ These find patterns and redundancy in content — Base62 is blind to structure entirely\n⚠️ Tradeoff: Encoding vs Compression\n→ Encoding (Base62) = changes representation, never reduces size of text\n→ Compression (gzip etc.) = reduces size by exploiting patterns, but output is binary (not human-readable)\n\n\nOne-liner\n\n→ Base62 makes numbers shorter to represent — applied to text it makes things longer. For actual size reduction, use compression.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
