      "locked": false
    },
    {
      "id": "Qyv-1lS4eFtcpduFStBRf",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -1105.5196744529867,
      "y": 3922.974844927745,
      "width": 809.6932983398438,
      "height": 3025,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2H",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 515503920,
      "version": 2542,
      "versionNonce": 809929759,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1779886751964,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "🎲 Regular RNG (e.g. Math.random())\n\nPseudorandom — starts from a seed and runs a deterministic algorithm (like a Mersenne\nTwister or xorshift). Given the same seed, it produces the same sequence every time.\n\n→ ⚡ Fast and cheap to compute\n→ 🔓 Predictable: if an attacker observes enough outputs, they can reconstruct the\ninternal state and predict future values\n→ 🕐 Seed is usually just the current timestamp\n→ 🎮 Fine for games, simulations, shuffling a playlist\n\n\n\n🔐 Cryptographic RNG (e.g. crypto.getRandomValues(), crypto.randomBytes())\n\nPulls entropy from genuinely unpredictable physical sources — CPU timing jitter, mouse\nmovement, hardware noise, OS-level entropy pools (/dev/urandom on Linux).\n\n→ 🐢 Slower, but still very fast in practice\n→ 🌀 Unpredictable: even if you see a thousand outputs, you cannot predict the next one\n→ 🚫 No reconstructible internal state\n→ 🛡️ What you use for passwords, tokens, session IDs, encryption keys\n\n\n\n⚠️ Why it matters for short code generation\n\nIf you use Math.random() to generate short link codes like abc123, an attacker could:\n\n→ 👀 Request a bunch of links, observe the generated codes\n→ 🔓 Reconstruct the RNG state\n→ 🔮 Predict what codes will be generated next — including codes belonging to other\nusers' private links\n\nWith crypto.getRandomValues(), each output is independent and unguessable, so there's\nno pattern to exploit.\n\n❌ Predictable\nMath.random().toString(36).slice(2, 8)\n\n✅ Unpredictable\nconst arr = new Uint8Array(4);\ncrypto.getRandomValues(arr);\n\n💡 The short version: regular RNG is random-looking, cryptographic RNG is random-\nbeing\n\n\n\n🧠 How RNG State Reconstruction Works\n\n🔢 Pseudorandom algorithms are just math — they take a state, apply a formula, output a\nnumber, and update the state. If you can reverse the formula, you can walk backwards\nfrom outputs to state.\n\n\n📐 Example: LCG (Simple, oldest RNG)\n\nnext_state = (a × current_state + c) % m\noutput = next_state\n\n→ ⚠️ a, c, m are known public constants — if you see one output, you already know the\nfull state because the output is the state. You can predict every future value.\n\n\n🌀 Example: Mersenne Twister (used by many languages)\n\n→ 📦 Internal state is an array of 624 integers — each output leaks one integer via a\nknown transformation called \"tempering\"\n→ 1️⃣ Collect 624 outputs\n→ 2️⃣ Reverse the tempering transformation on each (it's invertible math)\n→ 3️⃣ You've fully reconstructed the internal state array\n→ 4️⃣ You can now call nextValue() yourself and perfectly predict all future outputs\n\noutput = temper(state[i])\n         ↕ reversible\nstate[i] = untemper(output)\n\n\n💥 Why Math.random() (xorshift128+) is especially vulnerable\n\n→ 🧩 V8 (Chrome/Node) uses xorshift128+, which has only 128 bits of state\n→ 😬 Researchers have shown you only need ~2-3 outputs to fully reconstruct it\n→ 🛠️ Public tools like seed-recovery do this automatically\n\n\n🛡️ Why CRNG is immune\n\n→ 🔒 No reconstructible state — designed with forward secrecy\n→ ♻️ State is continuously re-seeded from fresh hardware entropy\n→ 🎲 Each output is a fresh draw from physical randomness, not derived from previous\nones\n\n\n🌐 Does the State Get Reused in a URL Shortener?\n\n🖥️ In Node.js (realistic)\n→ 📍 V8 initializes Math.random()'s state once on server start\n→ ☠️ Every call across every request draws from that same single continuous state\n\nRequest 1 → Math.random() → state advances 📍\nRequest 2 → Math.random() → state advances 📍\nRequest 3 → Math.random() → state advances 📍\n...all connected, all predictable once you have enough outputs\n\n\n💀 The Realistic Attack\n\nAttacker creates link → gets \"x7k2p1\" 👀\nAttacker creates link → gets \"m9q4r6\" 👀\nAttacker creates link → gets \"b3n8w5\" 👀\n// reconstructs state 🔓\n// predicts codes generated between their requests\n// those belong to other users' private links 😬\n\n\n🔑 Key Point\n\n→ 🌍 There is only one global state for the whole process — every output is part of the\nsame predictable sequence regardless of which request triggered it\n→ ✅ This is why short code generation warrants crypto.randomBytes()",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "R4nDOFOZykRsfvP00h0HT",
      "originalText": "🎲 Regular RNG (e.g. Math.random())\n\nPseudorandom — starts from a seed and runs a deterministic algorithm (like a Mersenne Twister or xorshift). Given the same seed, it produces the same sequence every time.\n\n→ ⚡ Fast and cheap to compute\n→ 🔓 Predictable: if an attacker observes enough outputs, they can reconstruct the internal state and predict future values\n→ 🕐 Seed is usually just the current timestamp\n→ 🎮 Fine for games, simulations, shuffling a playlist\n\n\n\n🔐 Cryptographic RNG (e.g. crypto.getRandomValues(), crypto.randomBytes())\n\nPulls entropy from genuinely unpredictable physical sources — CPU timing jitter, mouse movement, hardware noise, OS-level entropy pools (/dev/urandom on Linux).\n\n→ 🐢 Slower, but still very fast in practice\n→ 🌀 Unpredictable: even if you see a thousand outputs, you cannot predict the next one\n→ 🚫 No reconstructible internal state\n→ 🛡️ What you use for passwords, tokens, session IDs, encryption keys\n\n\n\n⚠️ Why it matters for short code generation\n\nIf you use Math.random() to generate short link codes like abc123, an attacker could:\n\n→ 👀 Request a bunch of links, observe the generated codes\n→ 🔓 Reconstruct the RNG state\n→ 🔮 Predict what codes will be generated next — including codes belonging to other users' private links\n\nWith crypto.getRandomValues(), each output is independent and unguessable, so there's no pattern to exploit.\n\n❌ Predictable\nMath.random().toString(36).slice(2, 8)\n\n✅ Unpredictable\nconst arr = new Uint8Array(4);\ncrypto.getRandomValues(arr);\n\n💡 The short version: regular RNG is random-looking, cryptographic RNG is random-being\n\n\n\n🧠 How RNG State Reconstruction Works\n\n🔢 Pseudorandom algorithms are just math — they take a state, apply a formula, output a number, and update the state. If you can reverse the formula, you can walk backwards from outputs to state.\n\n\n📐 Example: LCG (Simple, oldest RNG)\n\nnext_state = (a × current_state + c) % m\noutput = next_state\n\n→ ⚠️ a, c, m are known public constants — if you see one output, you already know the full state because the output is the state. You can predict every future value.\n\n\n🌀 Example: Mersenne Twister (used by many languages)\n\n→ 📦 Internal state is an array of 624 integers — each output leaks one integer via a known transformation called \"tempering\"\n→ 1️⃣ Collect 624 outputs\n→ 2️⃣ Reverse the tempering transformation on each (it's invertible math)\n→ 3️⃣ You've fully reconstructed the internal state array\n→ 4️⃣ You can now call nextValue() yourself and perfectly predict all future outputs\n\noutput = temper(state[i])\n         ↕ reversible\nstate[i] = untemper(output)\n\n\n💥 Why Math.random() (xorshift128+) is especially vulnerable\n\n→ 🧩 V8 (Chrome/Node) uses xorshift128+, which has only 128 bits of state\n→ 😬 Researchers have shown you only need ~2-3 outputs to fully reconstruct it\n→ 🛠️ Public tools like seed-recovery do this automatically\n\n\n🛡️ Why CRNG is immune\n\n→ 🔒 No reconstructible state — designed with forward secrecy\n→ ♻️ State is continuously re-seeded from fresh hardware entropy\n→ 🎲 Each output is a fresh draw from physical randomness, not derived from previous ones\n\n\n🌐 Does the State Get Reused in a URL Shortener?\n\n🖥️ In Node.js (realistic)\n→ 📍 V8 initializes Math.random()'s state once on server start\n→ ☠️ Every call across every request draws from that same single continuous state\n\nRequest 1 → Math.random() → state advances 📍\nRequest 2 → Math.random() → state advances 📍\nRequest 3 → Math.random() → state advances 📍\n...all connected, all predictable once you have enough outputs\n\n\n💀 The Realistic Attack\n\nAttacker creates link → gets \"x7k2p1\" 👀\nAttacker creates link → gets \"m9q4r6\" 👀\nAttacker creates link → gets \"b3n8w5\" 👀\n// reconstructs state 🔓\n// predicts codes generated between their requests\n// those belong to other users' private links 😬\n\n\n🔑 Key Point\n\n→ 🌍 There is only one global state for the whole process — every output is part of the same predictable sequence regardless of which request triggered it\n→ ✅ This is why short code generation warrants crypto.randomBytes()",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
