      "seed": 1938564843,
      "version": 2433,
      "versionNonce": 2037804747,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1775273411204,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "HMAC vs SHA-256\n\nSHA-256 is a hash function — it takes input and produces a fixed 256-bit fingerprint.\n\n→ 🔓 No secret key involved — anyone can compute it\n→ 📦 Same input always produces same output\n→ 🔍 Used for checksums, data integrity, storing passwords (with salt)\n\nHMAC (Hash-Based Message Authentication Code) is SHA-256 (or another hash) with a\nsecret key mixed in.\nHMAC(key, message) = SHA-256(key + SHA-256(key + message))\n\n→ 🔑 Requires a secret key to produce OR verify the output\n→ 🛡️ Without the key, you can't forge a valid HMAC even if you know the message\n→ ✅ Used for API authentication, signed tokens, verifying message integrity\n\n🤔 Key Difference\n\n                                            SHA-256                HMAC\n\nSecret key                               ❌                          ✅\nAnyone can compute            ✅                          ❌\nForgeable                               ✅                          ❌\nUse case                           Integrity             Authentication\n\n💡 Simple Analogy\n\n→ 🔢 SHA-256 is a public formula — anyone can run it\n→ 🔏 HMAC is SHA-256 locked with a secret — only parties with the key can produce or\nverify the output\n\nSo if you just need a fingerprint of data, SHA-256. If you need to prove the message came\nfrom someone with a secret key, HMAC.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "7rL4Zj4BaKNa1HM2lXhy8",
      "originalText": "HMAC vs SHA-256\n\nSHA-256 is a hash function — it takes input and produces a fixed 256-bit fingerprint.\n\n→ 🔓 No secret key involved — anyone can compute it\n→ 📦 Same input always produces same output\n→ 🔍 Used for checksums, data integrity, storing passwords (with salt)\n\nHMAC (Hash-Based Message Authentication Code) is SHA-256 (or another hash) with a secret key mixed in.\nHMAC(key, message) = SHA-256(key + SHA-256(key + message))\n\n→ 🔑 Requires a secret key to produce OR verify the output\n→ 🛡️ Without the key, you can't forge a valid HMAC even if you know the message\n→ ✅ Used for API authentication, signed tokens, verifying message integrity\n\n🤔 Key Difference\n\n                                            SHA-256                HMAC\n\nSecret key                               ❌                          ✅\nAnyone can compute            ✅                          ❌\nForgeable                               ✅                          ❌\nUse case                           Integrity             Authentication\n\n💡 Simple Analogy\n\n→ 🔢 SHA-256 is a public formula — anyone can run it\n→ 🔏 HMAC is SHA-256 locked with a secret — only parties with the key can produce or verify the output\n\nSo if you just need a fingerprint of data, SHA-256. If you need to prove the message came from someone with a secret key, HMAC.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
