      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Why crypto.randomBytes Has Larger Entropy Than Math.random()\n\n\n→ Both methods produce numbers from a seed — the key difference is where that seed\ncomes from\n\n\n🌱 Math.random() — Predictable Seed\n\n→ Uses a PRNG algorithm (e.g. xorshift128+ in V8/Chrome)\n→ Seed typically comes from something simple like the current timestamp\n→ Once the seed is set, the entire future sequence is mathematically determined\n→ Seed size: ~64 bits → only 2⁶⁴ possible sequences ever\n\n\n🔐 crypto.randomBytes() — Unpredictable Seed\n\n→ Uses a CSPRNG (Cryptographically Secure PRNG)\n→ Seed gathered from real-world physical randomness via an entropy pool:\nSourceExampleCPU timing jittertiny variations in processor speedHardware\ninterruptskeyboard presses, mouse movesDisk I/O timinghard drive read/write delaysOS-\nlevel randomness/dev/urandom on Linux\n→ These sources are physically unpredictable and constantly changing\n→ Seed size: 256+ bits → 2²⁵⁶ possible sequences\n\n\n↔️ Tradeoff: Randomness vs Performance\n\n                                            Math.random()             crypto.randomBytes()\nSeed source           Timestamp / simple value     Hardware entropy pool\nSeed size                             ~64 bits                                 256+ bits\nPredictable?          Yes, if seed is known               Practically impossible\nSpeed                                  ⚡ Fast                     Slower (entropy harvesting)\nUse case                      Games, simulations        Security, tokens, unique IDs\n\n→ Math.random() is fine when predictability doesn't matter — it's cheaper and fast\n→ Never use Math.random() for security-sensitive contexts (auth tokens, session IDs,\ncrypto keys) ⚠️\n\n\n🌌 How Much Bigger is 2²⁵⁶?\n\n→ 2⁶⁴ ≈ 18 quintillion\n→ 2²⁵⁶ ≈ a number with 77 digits — more than atoms in the observable universe\n→ Brute-forcing a crypto.randomBytes() seed is computationally impossible with current\nor foreseeable hardware\n\n\nOne-liner\n\n→ Math.random() derives randomness from a simple, guessable seed —\ncrypto.randomBytes() harvests it from chaotic, physical hardware events, making its\nentropy space incomparably larger",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "HRFs3cc8rZ5z7Cc7Nvx-J",
      "originalText": "Why crypto.randomBytes Has Larger Entropy Than Math.random()\n\n\n→ Both methods produce numbers from a seed — the key difference is where that seed comes from\n\n\n🌱 Math.random() — Predictable Seed\n\n→ Uses a PRNG algorithm (e.g. xorshift128+ in V8/Chrome)\n→ Seed typically comes from something simple like the current timestamp\n→ Once the seed is set, the entire future sequence is mathematically determined\n→ Seed size: ~64 bits → only 2⁶⁴ possible sequences ever\n\n\n🔐 crypto.randomBytes() — Unpredictable Seed\n\n→ Uses a CSPRNG (Cryptographically Secure PRNG)\n→ Seed gathered from real-world physical randomness via an entropy pool:\nSourceExampleCPU timing jittertiny variations in processor speedHardware interruptskeyboard presses, mouse movesDisk I/O timinghard drive read/write delaysOS-level randomness/dev/urandom on Linux\n→ These sources are physically unpredictable and constantly changing\n→ Seed size: 256+ bits → 2²⁵⁶ possible sequences\n\n\n↔️ Tradeoff: Randomness vs Performance\n\n                                            Math.random()             crypto.randomBytes()\nSeed source           Timestamp / simple value     Hardware entropy pool\nSeed size                             ~64 bits                                 256+ bits\nPredictable?          Yes, if seed is known               Practically impossible\nSpeed                                  ⚡ Fast                     Slower (entropy harvesting)\nUse case                      Games, simulations        Security, tokens, unique IDs\n\n→ Math.random() is fine when predictability doesn't matter — it's cheaper and fast\n→ Never use Math.random() for security-sensitive contexts (auth tokens, session IDs, crypto keys) ⚠️\n\n\n🌌 How Much Bigger is 2²⁵⁶?\n\n→ 2⁶⁴ ≈ 18 quintillion\n→ 2²⁵⁶ ≈ a number with 77 digits — more than atoms in the observable universe\n→ Brute-forcing a crypto.randomBytes() seed is computationally impossible with current or foreseeable hardware\n\n\nOne-liner\n\n→ Math.random() derives randomness from a simple, guessable seed — crypto.randomBytes() harvests it from chaotic, physical hardware events, making its entropy space incomparably larger",
