      "locked": false,
      "text": "Redis & PostgreSQL: Leader Election\n\n\n🐘 PostgreSQL — No Built-in Election\n\n→ Relies on external tools — most common is Patroni (uses etcd, Consul, or ZooKeeper)\n→ Patroni agents race to acquire a distributed lock — lock holder = leader\n→ Leader renews lock via heartbeats; if it stops, lock expires → others race to acquire it\n→ New primary promoted automatically, replicas re-pointed\n→ ⚠️ Requires external dependency (etcd/Consul) and failover takes seconds\n\n\n🔴 Redis Sentinel — Built-in, Instance-level\n\n→ Sentinel nodes monitor the primary and each other via heartbeats\n→ On failure, Sentinels vote — requires quorum majority to confirm primary is down\n→ One Sentinel is elected leader → promotes the replica with the most up-to-date replication offset\n→ ⚠️ Needs minimum 3 Sentinel nodes for reliable quorum\n→ ⚠️ Small data loss window if primary dies before replicating latest writes\n\n\n🔴 Redis Cluster — Built-in, Per-shard\n\n→ No Sentinel — election is fully self-contained via gossip protocol\n→ Each shard elects its own leader independently — multiple shards can failover simultaneously ✅\n→ On primary failure, replicas wait a random delay → request votes from other primaries → first to get\nmajority wins\n→ Cluster refuses writes if fewer than half the primaries are reachable — prevents split-brain ✅\n→ ⚠️ More complex — many moving parts across shards\n\nComparison\n\n\n                                   PostgreSQL (Patroni)                  Redis Sentinel                  Redis Cluster\nBuilt-in?                         ❌ External tool                               ✅                                     ✅\nConsensus via    etcd / Consul / ZooKeeper               Sentinel quorum         Gossip vote among primaries\nElection scope                Whole instance                    Whole instance                 Per shard\nExternal dependency     ✅ Required                           3+ Sentinel nodes             ❌ None\nSimultaneous failovers          ❌                                            ❌                                      ✅\nSplit-brain protection  Via consensus store                 Via quorum                   Refuses writes without majority\nData loss risk                 Minimal (sync replicas)           Small window                   Small window\n\n\nOne-liner\n\n→ Postgres outsources election to a consensus store; Sentinel uses a quorum vote across dedicated\nmonitor nodes; Cluster does it internally per shard via gossip — progressively more self-contained but\nmore complex",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "Lq7ALzX64Cc6D-l9thGmV",
      "originalText": "Redis & PostgreSQL: Leader Election\n\n\n🐘 PostgreSQL — No Built-in Election\n\n→ Relies on external tools — most common is Patroni (uses etcd, Consul, or ZooKeeper)\n→ Patroni agents race to acquire a distributed lock — lock holder = leader\n→ Leader renews lock via heartbeats; if it stops, lock expires → others race to acquire it\n→ New primary promoted automatically, replicas re-pointed\n→ ⚠️ Requires external dependency (etcd/Consul) and failover takes seconds\n\n\n🔴 Redis Sentinel — Built-in, Instance-level\n\n→ Sentinel nodes monitor the primary and each other via heartbeats\n→ On failure, Sentinels vote — requires quorum majority to confirm primary is down\n→ One Sentinel is elected leader → promotes the replica with the most up-to-date replication offset\n→ ⚠️ Needs minimum 3 Sentinel nodes for reliable quorum\n→ ⚠️ Small data loss window if primary dies before replicating latest writes\n\n\n🔴 Redis Cluster — Built-in, Per-shard\n\n→ No Sentinel — election is fully self-contained via gossip protocol\n→ Each shard elects its own leader independently — multiple shards can failover simultaneously ✅\n→ On primary failure, replicas wait a random delay → request votes from other primaries → first to get majority wins\n→ Cluster refuses writes if fewer than half the primaries are reachable — prevents split-brain ✅\n→ ⚠️ More complex — many moving parts across shards\n\nComparison\n\n\n                                   PostgreSQL (Patroni)                  Redis Sentinel                  Redis Cluster\nBuilt-in?                         ❌ External tool                               ✅                                     ✅\nConsensus via    etcd / Consul / ZooKeeper               Sentinel quorum         Gossip vote among primaries\nElection scope                Whole instance                    Whole instance                 Per shard\nExternal dependency     ✅ Required                           3+ Sentinel nodes             ❌ None\nSimultaneous failovers          ❌                                            ❌                                      ✅\nSplit-brain protection  Via consensus store                 Via quorum                   Refuses writes without majority\nData loss risk                 Minimal (sync replicas)           Small window                   Small window\n\n\nOne-liner\n\n→ Postgres outsources election to a consensus store; Sentinel uses a quorum vote across dedicated monitor nodes; Cluster does it internally per shard via gossip — progressively more self-contained but more complex",
